   "id": "spcpt_JC4Fp6MY98hw2Tgq",
   "name": "Common Acids",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Common Acids"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- An **acid** is a substance that **neutralises** a base forming a **salt** and **water**:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**acid + base **$\\rightleftharpoons$**salt + water**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**2HCl (aq) + CaO (s) **$\\rightleftharpoons$**CaCl**<sub>**2**</sub>** (aq) + H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O (l)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Acids are also substances that release **hydrogen ions** when they dissolve in water:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**HCl(g) → H**<sup>**+**</sup>**(aq) + Cl**<sup>**- **</sup>**(aq)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Acid dissociation"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2020/11/1.7-Equilibria-Acid-Dissociation.png",
   "file": "assets/ee190122870f-1-7-equilibria-acid-dissociation.png",
   "alt": "Chemical equation showing HA dissociating into H⁺ and A⁻ ions; HA in red and green circles, arrow indicating reaction direction.",
   "caption": "Acids dissociate in water to release a hydrogen ion"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Common acids"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Hydrochloric acid**\n\n  - HCl forms H<sup>+</sup> + Cl<sup>–</sup> in water\n- **Nitric acid**\n\n  - HNO<sub>3 </sub>forms H<sup>+</sup> + NO<sub>3</sub><sup>–</sup> in water\n- **Sulfuric acid**\n\n  - H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4 </sub>forms H<sup>+</sup> + SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2–</sup> in water\n- **Ethanoic acid**\n\n  - CH<sub>3</sub>COOH forms H<sup>+</sup> + CH<sub>3</sub>COO<sup>–</sup> in water"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Monoprotic inorganic acids, such as hydrochloric acid, fully dissociate into their ions\n- Organic acids, such as **carboxylic acids**, do not fully dissociate into their ions\n\n  - Only some of the hydrogen atoms can form ions"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_2zcRGY6kntsY6nJP",
   "name": "Common Alkalis",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Common Alkalis"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **base** is a compound that **neutralises **an acid forming a **salt **and **water**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**acid + base **$\\rightleftharpoons$**salt + water**"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "**2HCl (aq) + CaO (s) **$\\rightleftharpoons$**CaCl**<sub>**2**</sub>** (aq) + H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O (l)**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A base is a substance that **accepts** hydrogen ions or a compound that contains **oxide** or **hydroxide** ions\n\n  - For example, when the base ammonia is added to water, the ammonium ion and hydroxide ions are formed:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "NH<sub>3 </sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O (l) → NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+ </sup>(aq) + OH<sup>– </sup>(aq)"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- For example, when sodium hydroxide is dissolved in solution, sodium ions and hydroxide ions are formed:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "NaOH (s) + aq → Na<sup>+ </sup>(aq) + OH<sup>– </sup>(aq)"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A base that is **soluble **in water is called an **alkali**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Common alkalis table"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- **Sodium hydroxide**\n\n  - NaOH forms Na<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>–</sup> in water\n- **Potassium hydroxide**\n\n  - KOH forms K<sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>–</sup>  in water\n- **Aqueous ammonia**\n\n  - NH<sub>3</sub> forms NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup> + OH<sup>–</sup> in water"
  }
 ]
}